      "name": null,
      "problem": "Let $(v,w)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $v+w?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.4v-2w=2 \\\\ v-w=1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.4v-2w=2 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ v-w=1 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4v-20w=20 \\\\ v-w=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-4,$ then we will get a $-4v$ term that can cancel with the $4v$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4v-20w=20 &  \\\\ v-w=1 & |\\,\\times (-4)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4v-20w=20 \\\\ -4v+4w=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $v$-variable and allows us to solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4v}-20w & =20 \\\\ -\\cancel{4v}+4w & =-4 \\\\ (-20w+4w) & =(20-4) \\\\ -16w & =16 \\\\ w & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $w=-1$ into the equation $v-w=1$ to solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-w & =1 \\\\ v-(-1) & =1 \\\\ v+1 & =1 \\\\ v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=0,\\,w=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\nv+w=0+(-1)=-1.\n$$",
